{"name":"Concrete Landscape","hex":"#5c606e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"concrete","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Real, actual, tangible.","Analogous to the categories of algebraic objects which category theory was created to generalize, in the sense of having objects which can be thought of as sets equipped with some additional structure. Formally, equipped with a faithful functor to the category of sets."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A building material created by mixing cement, water, and aggregate such as gravel and sand.","Such a material whose cement is Portland cement or a similar limestone derivative."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/concrete"},{"term":"landscape","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A portion of land or territory as defined by its landform, its geographical (and architectural) features.","A portion of land which the eye can comprehend in a single view, including all the objects thus seen."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To create or maintain a landscape."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/landscape"}],"entries":[{"title":"Concrete","blocks":[{"text":"Concrete is a composite material composed of aggregate bound together with a fluid cement that cures to a solid. It is the second-most-used substance, the most widely used building material, and the most-manufactured material in the world. Cement-bound concrete differs from the less rigid, less durable asphaltic concrete, which has a bituminous binder."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Bloczek_betonowy.jpg/330px-Bloczek_betonowy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Concrete"},{"title":"Landscape","blocks":[{"text":"A landscape is the visible features of an area of land, its landforms, and how they integrate with natural or human-made features, often considered in terms of their aesthetic appeal. A landscape includes the physical elements of geophysically defined landforms such as mountains, hills, water bodies such as rivers, lakes, ponds and the sea, living elements of land cover including indigenous vegetation, human elements including different forms of land use, buildings, and structures, and transitory elements such as lighting and weather conditions. Combining both their physical origins and the cultural overlay of human presence, often created over millennia, landscapes reflect a living synthesis of people and place that is vital to local and national identity."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Cumulus_Clouds_over_Yellow_Prairie2.jpg/330px-Cumulus_Clouds_over_Yellow_Prairie2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Landscape"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Rome_%28Italy%2C_October_2019%29_-_275_%2850589571796%29.jpg/500px-Rome_%28Italy%2C_October_2019%29_-_275_%2850589571796%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Rome_%28Italy%2C_October_2019%29_-_275_%2850589571796%29.jpg/1280px-Rome_%28Italy%2C_October_2019%29_-_275_%2850589571796%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Rome_%28Italy%2C_October_2019%29_-_275_%2850589571796%29.jpg/500px-Rome_%28Italy%2C_October_2019%29_-_275_%2850589571796%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Exterior of the Roman Pantheon, finished 128 AD, the largest unreinforced concrete dome in the world.[16]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARome_(Italy%2C_October_2019)_-_275_(50589571796).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Pantheon_%28Rome%29_-_Dome.jpg/500px-Pantheon_%28Rome%29_-_Dome.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Pantheon_%28Rome%29_-_Dome.jpg/1280px-Pantheon_%28Rome%29_-_Dome.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Pantheon_%28Rome%29_-_Dome.jpg/500px-Pantheon_%28Rome%29_-_Dome.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Interior of the Pantheon dome, seen from beneath. The concrete for the coffered dome was laid on moulds, mounted on temporary scaffolding.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APantheon_(Rome)_-_Dome.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Museo_Foro_Caesaragusta_-_Cloaca_del_foro_03.JPG/500px-Museo_Foro_Caesaragusta_-_Cloaca_del_foro_03.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Museo_Foro_Caesaragusta_-_Cloaca_del_foro_03.JPG/1280px-Museo_Foro_Caesaragusta_-_Cloaca_del_foro_03.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Museo_Foro_Caesaragusta_-_Cloaca_del_foro_03.JPG/500px-Museo_Foro_Caesaragusta_-_Cloaca_del_foro_03.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Opus caementicium exposed in a characteristic Roman arch. In contrast to modern concrete structures, the concrete used in Roman buildings was usually covered with brick or stone.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMuseo_Foro_Caesaragusta_-_Cloaca_del_foro_03.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/61/Smeaton%27s_Lighthouse00.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/61/Smeaton%27s_Lighthouse00.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipe